<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory</h1><p class="st-meta">1943 CE – 1951 CE · capital Sabha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Libyan Fezzan under Free French military administration after its capture by Leclerc's forces from Chad, until Libyan independence in 1951.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In late 1942 and January 1943 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free French</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-leclerc-de-hauteclocque">Philippe Leclerc</a> advanced from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chad-french-colony">Chad (French colony)</a> and took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Fezzan</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-libya">Italian Libya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> administered it as a military territory together with the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a> as its centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French officers governed in cooperation with the Sayf al-Nasr family, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sayf-al-nasr">Ahmad Sayf al-Nasr</a> acted as chief of the territory. Parts were administratively linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> hoped to keep the region to secure links between its North and Central African colonies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> decided in 1949 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> should become independent. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Fezzan</a> joined the federal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-libya">Kingdom of Libya</a> at independence in December 1951 as one of its three provinces, though French troops stayed until 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/fezzan-ghadames-military-territory.jpg" alt="Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1943 CE – 1951 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sabha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe Leclerc, Ahmad Sayf al-Nasr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Tebu, Berber, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Fezzan, Territoire militaire du Fezzan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=fezzan-ghadames-military-territory" aria-label="Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 1186 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5380 1442L5338 1239M5380 1442L5054 728M5380 1442L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5054 728h0M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 1442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sabha. <a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=fezzan-ghadames-military-territory">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
